WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.030
Hundreds and thousands of people

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.838
are gonna lose their homes. They will die

00:00:03.838 --> 00:00:06.380
Let's be honest: for a
cruel and desperate hope

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.450
of maximizing profits.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.210
Stop evictions at all costs.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.880
They brought living room furniture

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.030
into the streets,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.300
saying this is what
the city will look like

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.740
as thousands face eviction.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.710
Their demand: cancel the rent.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.640
A lot of folks have had to choose

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.940
whether to eat or to pay rent.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.230
The federal government
and many cities and states

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.410
have enacted a series
of temporary measures,

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.650
halting residential evictions
in the United States.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.785
Evictions were leading to
unnecessary cases of COVID,

00:00:36.785 --> 00:00:39.100
and unnecessary deaths from COVID.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.280
Dubbed "the tenant
movement's giant killer"

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.100
by one industry trade magazine,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.350
Cea Weaver is a rising
star in New York politics.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.810
So this is life saving,

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.813
and that's the most important thing.

00:00:49.813 --> 00:00:51.410
The cancel rent movement,

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.757
Weaver told the New York Times

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:55.940
"is also about literally rising
up for real transformation

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.390
in the housing market."

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.390
In New York, that
transformation is underway,

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.460
and it's having a devastating
impact on landlords.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.320
The eviction moratorium isn't
just benefiting the poor;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.930
it's allowing bad actors
to stop paying their rent

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.340
without consequence.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.980
Meanwhile, many small landlords

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.200
are seeing their life savings disappear,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.410
because they have no choice

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.475
but to maintain their buildings at a loss.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.520
One landlord who's been hurt
by the eviction moratorium

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.110
is Chao Huai Gao, who
works at a nail salon

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.430
and saved for 20 years
to buy a house in Queens.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.900
In March of last year,
a squatter moved in.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.980
This person, who didn't respond
to our interview request,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.640
is a dropout from an
elite private university,

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.520
and he has never paid rent.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.370
Because of the eviction moratorium,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.780
there's no legal mechanism

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.710
through which Gao can retake
possession of his property.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:59.711
Meanwhile, his bills are mounting.

00:02:02.011 --> 00:02:04.110
He no pay rent, I so angry.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.343
Is a robbery.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.617
So, in my story one landlord says,

00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:25.590
This is really a straightforward
issue, it's "robbery."

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.086
He's paying heat taxes and a mortgage,

00:02:28.086 --> 00:02:30.340
and someone else is living rent-free

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.080
and he has absolutely no recourse.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.530
Do you agree with that
characterization? Is this robbery?

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.440
No, I don't think it's robbery
and we're also not saying

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.330
that the landlord can
never collect the rent-

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.030
that's not what an
eviction moratorium does.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.910
It's simply a pause.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.820
So, no I don't agree with it,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.710
I think it's deeply misleading.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.510
But practically speaking,

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.620
this landlord will be very lucky,

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.530
I mean, just the way housing court runs,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.890
if he is able to collect back rent

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.230
in terms of the cost of hiring lawyers,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.570
the delays that come in housing court.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.880
You don't have to be in the
business of being a landlord.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.580
Those are part of the
costs of being a landlord.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.452
That should be understood as
part of the deal, and it is.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.600
But owners say having
almost no legal recourse

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.390
when their tenants don't pay their rent

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.020
is not something they considered
to be part of the deal

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.582
when investing in real estate.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.840
Landlord groups around
the country have sued

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.110
on the grounds that halting
the judicial process

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.040
that allows them to retake their property

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.960
violates their due process rights,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.370
and that the national moratorium

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.820
is an unconstitutional
expansion of federal power.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.440
Gao is part of an association

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.020
of about 200 Chinese immigrant landlords,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.159
with tenants who have stopped paying rent.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.630
This landlord worked as
a housekeeper at a hotel

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.150
that recently closed down.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.840
She says her tenants stopped
paying rent over the summer,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.426
and is demanding a $12,000
cash payment to move out.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.210
I think it's really critical
that mom-and-pop landlords

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.110
are prioritized for aid right now.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.790
At the same time, everyone
has taken a haircut,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.540
they don't have a God-given
right to collect rent

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.790
just like hotels don't
have a God-given right

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:35.950
to profit off of the tourism industry,

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:37.950
and bars and restaurants
don't have a God-given right

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.292
to serve people dinner and wine.

00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:41.125
What do we want?

00:04:41.125 --> 00:04:41.958
Cancel our rent.

00:04:41.958 --> 00:04:42.861
When do we want it?

00:04:42.861 --> 00:04:43.730
Now.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:44.589
If we don't get it?

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:45.583
Shut it down.

00:04:45.583 --> 00:04:47.860
If we don't get it?

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.150
Weaver said she feels for these landlords,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.830
but tenants are worse off.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:54.750
And we know as a matter
of statistical fact

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.230
that renters are far less
likely to have wealth,

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.960
and renters are far less likely
to have anything in saving.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.450
I know that there's some examples,

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.610
like the ones that you're raising,

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.310
and I feel for that person

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:06.640
and want them to get the resources

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:07.970
that they need from the state.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:09.710
The media has predicted a huge wave

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.400
of evictions in the United States

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.030
if the moratoriums are lifted,

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.460
and in New York tenant activists

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.180
dragged furniture out into the streets

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.340
to dramatize the potential impact.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.920
But, in New York, before COVID,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.170
only about one out of 10 eviction filings

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.990
ended in a city marshal or sheriff

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.330
physically removing a
tenant from a dwelling.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.340
However, because there existed a process

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.530
to which landlords could
enforce their contracts

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.370
and get tenants who
weren't paying their rent

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.330
to come to the negotiating
table or move out,

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.820
they had legal recourse.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.950
One problem with the eviction moratorium

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.870
is that in practice it's not
only impacting those in need,

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:43.550
it's also allowing tenants
who can afford their rent

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.510
to take advantage of their landlords.

00:05:52.543 --> 00:05:55.113
Won, who asked that we
only use her last name,

00:05:55.113 --> 00:05:57.330
owns a house in Queens and
works as a home health aid

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:58.860
caring for an elderly couple.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.120
Her husband is a waiter at
this restaurant in Chinatown,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.820
though he's barely been able
to work since the pandemic.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.280
She says that her tenant owes her

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.760
more than $80,000 in back rent.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.990
Won says that for tenants
who were unemployed

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.240
or financially distressed,

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.960
she'd be willing to work with them.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:35.220
Reason wasn't able to reach
her tenants for comment,

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.340
so we're not using their
names in the story.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.200
But we did find that the father
who has a criminal record,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.620
is currently employed in
the construction industry.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.560
And while his girlfriend's
mother and children

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.910
live in Won's apartment,

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.080
he and his girlfriend are
renting a second apartment

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.710
in this house in Queens Village,

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.300
where they've also stopped paying rent

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.957
and owe $12,000 to the
couple that owns the house,

00:06:53.957 --> 00:06:55.910
who are also Chinese immigrants.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.840
But it's not only immigrant landlords

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.500
who are being taken advantage of.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.840
Sharon Redhead, who's the
owner of a building in Brooklyn

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.970
told the New York Daily News
that some of her tenants

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:05.790
who were out of work have
kept up with their rent,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.180
while others with jobs aren't
paying any rent at all.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:10.540
Clarence Hamer is the
owner of a two-family home

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.080
with a tenant who isn't paying rent,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.020
but is subletting rooms at a profit.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.310
The New York Post told the story

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.980
of 88-year-old Harlem
landlord David Howson,

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.510
suffering from Alzheimer's,

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:22.587
with a tenant who hasn't
paid a dime since 2016.

00:07:22.587 --> 00:07:24.270
"We're completely destitute,"

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.770
his daughter told The Post.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.490
The idea that there's a handful of renters

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.857
who are maybe working and saying like,

00:07:32.857 --> 00:07:35.190
"Oh, I have the money and I'm
just not gonna pay right now"-

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.040
All of the data is showing
that that's an aberration.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.610
The data that shows people
who are borrowing to pay rent,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:44.100
the data that shows that
renters 48% of renters

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.360
are behind on their housing costs

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.020
compared to 6% of property owners

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:48.424
who are behind on their housing costs.

00:07:48.424 --> 00:07:51.670
The data is just with us.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.620
Weaver's referring to census data

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.310
that doesn't single out landlords,

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.670
but that does show that renters

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.150
are more financially distressed

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.030
than people who own their own homes.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:01.870
And newspapers have recounted the stories

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.670
of many tenants in
crisis such as Diba Gaye,

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.630
whose wife recently died of heart disease,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.807
and who lost his job stocking groceries.

00:08:08.807 --> 00:08:10.240
"I don't wanna lose my house too,"

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.450
he told The Times.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:14.620
And Halima Abdul-Wahhab, who
says she and her two children

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:15.997
are at risk of homelessness.

00:08:15.997 --> 00:08:18.020
"I'm at a job where I
don't make that much,

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.897
but I just try to maintain
as much as I can," she said.

00:08:20.897 --> 00:08:22.340
"Rent is not the only thing

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.010
that has to be paid every month."

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:25.690
But these individuals can be helped

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.210
with the type of direct aid

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.610
that the government has been providing

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.620
since the early days of the pandemic,

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:32.830
through interest free
loan, stimulus checks,

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:35.840
and a substantial increase
in unemployment benefits.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.060
Cities and states have also spent billions

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:40.620
in federal money on rent relief programs.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.750
These programs bring
their own set of problems

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.040
and trade-offs, and those
funded by philanthropy

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.960
have proven more effective
than government financed ones.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.130
But if tenants are
receiving the aid they need,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.390
owners should still be
able to turn to the courts

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:53.680
if they don't pay their rent.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.250
Weaver says this approach
fails to help populations

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.270
that are hard to reach with direct aid,

00:08:58.270 --> 00:08:59.930
like undocumented immigrants.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.940
Her organization helped to
draft a proposed state law

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.070
under which the government
would cover missing rent,

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.280
no matter the recent tenants hadn't paid,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.680
and with strings attached for
landlords to accept the money.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:11.640
Because we think it'll get the money out

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:12.706
the fastest, right.

00:09:12.706 --> 00:09:14.840
The landlords have rent ledgers,

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.380
they have operating statements,
they have rent rolls.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:19.780
But there's another reason Weaver

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:20.950
and her political allies

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.760
want to make landlords
dependent on federal subsidies.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.680
Emergency policies
enacted in times of crisis

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.390
are prone to becoming permanent,

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.320
which some members of
the cancel rent movement

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.420
say is the goal.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:35.510
We need to think about the
role that exclusion and profit,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.170
which are the sort of characteristics

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:40.330
of the private property
market have played,

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:42.340
and think about different
systems and structures

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:43.730
that we could put into place

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.980
that would help more people be housed.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:46.930
Weaver would like to see

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.780
the entire real estate industry

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.460
restructured in a model
akin to public housing,

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.810
but for rich people too.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:55.510
In public housing, people are
paying 30% of their income.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.560
What I am envisioning is a world

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:00.540
in which the housing is
owned by a collective,

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.800
and people are paying 30% of their income

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:03.900
in order to live in their housing.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.500
If your income is zero you pay zero,

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.600
if your income is $500,000 a year,

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.500
you're paying 30% of that.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.450
And the government is the sort of owner-

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.100
or not even the owner.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.420
The government doesn't
have to be the owner

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:20.110
but the government is what's making sure

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.990
all of that sort of works in cash flows.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.920
The debt to GDP ratio
right now is the highest

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.190
since World War II,

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:31.680
Trump left us with an additional
$7.8 trillion in debt,

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.350
and Biden's agenda would add 11 trillion

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:35.700
on top of what we're already spending

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:36.740
over the next 10 years,

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.930
so how can the federal
government also afford

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.467
to start subsidizing rental housing costs?

00:10:43.467 --> 00:10:44.760
The federal government prints money.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.770
The federal government can
provide money for this.

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:49.230
So it's by printing money?

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:50.316
Sure.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.310
Gao, his wife and two daughters

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.050
are living in a one-room studio,

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.930
unable to move into the building they own.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.600
And they're afraid to contact
the person squatting there

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.104
at a fear that there'll
be sued for harassment.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:19.720
When Gao and other immigrant landlords

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.490
decided to come to the United States

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:22.970
to create a better life,

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.940
this is not the American dream

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.114
they thought they were buying into.

